---
import PrepLayout from '../../layouts/PrepLayout.astro';
---

<PrepLayout
  title="Free ML interview prep tools"
  description="Private, browser-only tools and practical worksheets for senior ML interview preparation."
  heading="Turn the library into a practice system."
  lede="Use these free tools to choose a focus, practice closed-book, organize experience stories, and structure the time you have. No account or personal profile required."
>
  <div class="resource-grid">
    <a class="resource-card" href="/readiness/">
      <h2>Readiness check</h2>
      <p>Identify three broad focus areas and choose a 2-, 4-, or 8-week starting plan.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="resource-card" href="/practice/">
      <h2>Practice method</h2>
      <p>Use closed-book attempts, a five-part rubric, and delayed retries instead of rereading.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="resource-card" href="/story-bank/">
      <h2>Story-bank worksheet</h2>
      <p>Build a compact set of evidence-rich behavioral and project stories.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="resource-card" href="/plans/">
      <h2>Generic prep plans</h2>
      <p>Pick a realistic 2-, 4-, or 8-week cadence and adapt it to your interview loop.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="resource-card" href="/final-week/">
      <h2>Final-week checklist</h2>
      <p>Consolidate, simulate, and protect retrieval quality without cramming new topics.</p>
    </a>
    <a class="resource-card" href="/questions/">
      <h2>Question library</h2>
      <p>Start Practice Mode directly from any of the level-calibrated interview questions.</p>
    </a>
  </div>

  <section class="callout">
    <h2>Privacy by design</h2>
    <p>The readiness calculation runs in your browser. Practice scratchpad text is never saved or sent. Story-bank and checklist text stays on the current page unless you print it. Anonymous analytics record only coarse actions such as starting a practice attempt—not selections or written content.</p>
  </section>

  <h2>A simple sequence</h2>
  <ol class="step-list">
    <li><strong>Choose a focus.</strong> Take the readiness check once; do not keep retaking it to chase a better result.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose a horizon.</strong> Use the shortest plan that still leaves room for retries and one full-loop simulation.</li>
    <li><strong>Practice before reading.</strong> Attempt questions aloud, reveal the rubric, then compare against the reference.</li>
    <li><strong>Consolidate.</strong> Revisit weak attempts after two days and finish with the final-week checklist.</li>
  </ol>
</PrepLayout>
